1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,958
A doctor checks your pulse three times.

2
00:00:02,958 --> 00:00:05,122
A tailor measures a sleeve twice.

3
00:00:05,122 --> 00:00:06,549
A cook tastes twice.

4
00:00:06,949 --> 00:00:09,533
Why measure the same
thing again and again?

5
00:00:09,533 --> 00:00:11,552
Because no measurement is perfect.

6
00:00:12,002 --> 00:00:16,575
Diya pours one glass of water
into a measuring jug, five times.

7
00:00:16,925 --> 00:00:18,253
Two forty eight.

8
00:00:18,253 --> 00:00:19,473
Two fifty two.

9
00:00:19,473 --> 00:00:20,693
Two fifty one.

10
00:00:20,693 --> 00:00:22,020
Two forty seven.

11
00:00:22,020 --> 00:00:23,953
Two fifty two millilitres.

12
00:00:24,453 --> 00:00:26,228
Same glass, five numbers.

13
00:00:26,228 --> 00:00:27,617
Which one is right?

14
00:00:27,617 --> 00:00:29,209
And how wrong is each?

15
00:00:29,859 --> 00:00:31,892
Every reading has a small error.

16
00:00:31,892 --> 00:00:35,269
The error is how far a reading
is from the true value.

17
00:00:35,619 --> 00:00:37,970
We never know the true value exactly.

18
00:00:37,970 --> 00:00:40,955
Our best guess is the
mean of all the readings.

19
00:00:41,405 --> 00:00:44,463
The five add up to
twelve hundred and fifty.

20
00:00:44,463 --> 00:00:45,670
Divide by five.

21
00:00:45,670 --> 00:00:48,768
The mean is two hundred
and fifty millilitres.

22
00:00:49,418 --> 00:00:51,976
Let's put the readings
on our measuring bench.

23
00:00:52,326 --> 00:00:55,694
Each pour fills the jug to
a slightly different mark.

24
00:00:55,694 --> 00:00:56,877
Watch the level.

25
00:00:57,277 --> 00:00:59,881
On the graph, each reading becomes a dot.

26
00:00:59,881 --> 00:01:02,394
The dashed line is the mean, two fifty.

27
00:01:02,844 --> 00:01:06,791
The gap between a dot and the mean
is that reading's absolute error.

28
00:01:07,241 --> 00:01:09,149
Slow replay, reading one.

29
00:01:09,149 --> 00:01:12,303
Two forty eight minus
two fifty is minus two.

30
00:01:12,303 --> 00:01:13,641
Its size is two.

31
00:01:14,091 --> 00:01:16,707
The other gaps are two,
one, three and two.

32
00:01:16,707 --> 00:01:19,274
Some readings are above
the mean, some below.

33
00:01:19,924 --> 00:01:22,849
Let's turn this into
rules, one step at a time.

34
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:24,100
Step one.

35
00:01:23,995 --> 00:01:28,099
The mean, a bar, is the sum
of the readings, divided by n.

36
00:01:28,849 --> 00:01:29,749
Step two.

37
00:01:29,663 --> 00:01:34,274
The absolute error of each reading,
delta a i, is the reading minus the

38
00:01:34,274 --> 00:01:35,174
mean.

39
00:01:35,581 --> 00:01:36,560
Step three.

40
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:41,274
The mean absolute error is the
average of the sizes of these errors.

41
00:01:41,274 --> 00:01:43,349
Here, ten divided by five.

42
00:01:43,349 --> 00:01:44,594
Two millilitres.

43
00:01:45,344 --> 00:01:46,244
Step four.

44
00:01:46,196 --> 00:01:51,285
So we write the result as two hundred
and fifty, plus or minus two millilitres.

45
00:01:52,035 --> 00:01:56,710
The true value most likely lies between
two forty eight and two fifty two.

46
00:01:57,410 --> 00:02:00,410
But is two millilitres a
big error or a small one?

47
00:02:00,410 --> 00:02:02,311
It depends on what you measure.

48
00:02:02,661 --> 00:02:05,104
So compare the error with the value.

49
00:02:05,104 --> 00:02:09,416
Relative error equals the mean
absolute error, divided by the mean.

50
00:02:10,166 --> 00:02:14,248
Two divided by two fifty is
zero point zero zero eight.

51
00:02:14,998 --> 00:02:18,744
Multiply by one hundred, and
you get the percentage error.

52
00:02:18,744 --> 00:02:20,437
Zero point eight percent.

53
00:02:21,187 --> 00:02:22,263
Remember this.

54
00:02:22,263 --> 00:02:24,584
Absolute error tells you how much.

55
00:02:24,584 --> 00:02:28,253
Relative error tells you how
much compared to the value.

56
00:02:28,953 --> 00:02:30,008
Side by side.

57
00:02:30,008 --> 00:02:33,711
The glass: two hundred and
fifty millilitres, error two.

58
00:02:33,711 --> 00:02:35,919
That is zero point eight percent.

59
00:02:36,369 --> 00:02:40,313
A spoon of cough syrup: ten
millilitres, the same error of two.

60
00:02:40,313 --> 00:02:41,810
That is twenty percent.

61
00:02:42,410 --> 00:02:43,511
Now you try.

62
00:02:43,511 --> 00:02:45,040
Same absolute error.

63
00:02:45,040 --> 00:02:47,060
Which measurement is better?

64
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:48,428
Pause and decide.

65
00:02:51,628 --> 00:02:52,528
The glass!

66
00:02:52,411 --> 00:02:54,831
Its error is a tiny part of the whole.

67
00:02:54,831 --> 00:02:58,681
For the syrup, the same two
millilitres is a fifth of the dose.

68
00:02:59,331 --> 00:03:01,214
One more thing about the units.

69
00:03:01,564 --> 00:03:04,692
Absolute error has the
same unit as the reading.

70
00:03:04,692 --> 00:03:05,839
Two millilitres.

71
00:03:06,289 --> 00:03:09,835
Relative error is millilitres
divided by millilitres.

72
00:03:09,835 --> 00:03:11,202
The units cancel.

73
00:03:11,202 --> 00:03:12,858
It is a pure number.

74
00:03:13,308 --> 00:03:14,208
Your turn.

75
00:03:14,179 --> 00:03:16,490
Write the glass in litres instead.

76
00:03:16,490 --> 00:03:18,692
Does the percentage error change?

77
00:03:18,692 --> 00:03:19,973
Pause and decide.

78
00:03:23,173 --> 00:03:24,073
No!

79
00:03:23,649 --> 00:03:28,290
Two thousandths of a litre over a
quarter litre is still zero point zero

80
00:03:28,290 --> 00:03:29,241
zero eight.

81
00:03:29,241 --> 00:03:31,412
Still zero point eight percent.

82
00:03:32,062 --> 00:03:33,290
Worked example.

83
00:03:33,290 --> 00:03:36,896
Diya times ten swings of
a pendulum, five times.

84
00:03:37,246 --> 00:03:41,746
Nineteen point eight, twenty point
two, twenty point one, nineteen point

85
00:03:41,746 --> 00:03:43,942
seven, twenty point two seconds.

86
00:03:44,442 --> 00:03:46,159
The sum is one hundred.

87
00:03:46,159 --> 00:03:48,967
So the mean is twenty point zero seconds.

88
00:03:49,717 --> 00:03:52,371
The error sizes add to one point zero.

89
00:03:52,371 --> 00:03:54,915
Their mean is zero point two seconds.

90
00:03:55,665 --> 00:03:58,661
Relative error, zero
point two over twenty.

91
00:03:58,661 --> 00:04:00,723
That is zero point zero one.

92
00:04:01,473 --> 00:04:06,450
So the time is twenty point zero,
plus or minus zero point two seconds.

93
00:04:06,450 --> 00:04:08,015
A one percent error.

94
00:04:08,665 --> 00:04:10,130
Now, the exam trap.

95
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:13,503
Many students average the
errors with their signs.

96
00:04:13,503 --> 00:04:17,152
Minus two, plus two, plus
one, minus three, plus two.

97
00:04:17,552 --> 00:04:21,379
That sum is always zero, because
the mean sits in the middle!

98
00:04:21,379 --> 00:04:22,810
Drop the signs first.

99
00:04:22,810 --> 00:04:25,532
Take the size of each error, then average.

100
00:04:26,182 --> 00:04:27,562
Where is this asked?

101
00:04:27,562 --> 00:04:28,886
As short numericals.

102
00:04:28,886 --> 00:04:33,230
From a set of readings, find the
mean, the mean absolute error, or the

103
00:04:33,230 --> 00:04:34,360
percentage error.

104
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:36,149
Exam level now.

105
00:04:36,149 --> 00:04:39,041
Five readings of a length, in centimetres.

106
00:04:39,041 --> 00:04:40,845
Find the percentage error.

107
00:04:40,845 --> 00:04:42,084
Pause and solve.

108
00:04:47,484 --> 00:04:48,863
The answer is C.

109
00:04:48,863 --> 00:04:50,722
Zero point seven percent.

110
00:04:51,222 --> 00:04:53,679
The mean is four point zero zero.

111
00:04:53,679 --> 00:04:58,435
The error sizes add to zero point
one four, so the mean absolute error

112
00:04:58,435 --> 00:05:00,617
is zero point zero two eight.

113
00:05:01,117 --> 00:05:06,243
Zero point zero two eight over four,
times one hundred, is zero point seven

114
00:05:06,243 --> 00:05:07,143
percent.

115
00:05:06,995 --> 00:05:09,282
Zero percent is the signs trap.

116
00:05:09,882 --> 00:05:11,341
Here is your recap.

117
00:05:11,341 --> 00:05:12,841
Screenshot this card.

118
00:05:16,041 --> 00:05:18,877
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

119
00:05:19,277 --> 00:05:20,177
One.

120
00:05:19,774 --> 00:05:23,604
Why do we use the mean of many
readings as the true value?

121
00:05:24,504 --> 00:05:25,404
Two.

122
00:05:24,990 --> 00:05:29,157
Why is the average of the errors,
with their signs, always zero?

123
00:05:30,057 --> 00:05:30,957
Three.

124
00:05:30,674 --> 00:05:33,916
Same absolute error on
a big and a small value.

125
00:05:33,916 --> 00:05:36,458
Which has the bigger percentage error?

126
00:05:37,558 --> 00:05:41,872
Next time: Aarav's study table has
a small error in its length and its

127
00:05:41,872 --> 00:05:42,772
width.

128
00:05:42,465 --> 00:05:44,573
What is the error in its area?

129
00:05:44,573 --> 00:05:45,807
Error propagation.
